\section{Stability and Determinism Theorems for CL-DIV}

\begin{theorem}[Deterministic Output Invariance]
Let the input state of the engine be defined by an initial condition vector 
$\bm{x}_0$, a refractive index field $n_{\text{eff}}(\bm{x},\lambda)$, and a 
random seed $\sigma$. Assume:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item All ray queues and scatter-gather operations are compacted via atomic
    prefix-sum sorting.
    \item The GPU pipeline, hardware telemetry vector $S$, and CPU shadow pass
    agree within a differential bound $|\Delta| < 10^{-7}$.
\end{enumerate}
Then the frame buffer output $F$ is bitwise deterministic:
\[
(\bm{x}_0, n_{\text{eff}}, \sigma) \Rightarrow F
\]
and repeated executions with identical $(\bm{x}_0, n_{\text{eff}}, \sigma)$
produce identical $F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Condition (1) removes nondeterminism due to parallel race conditions by enforcing
a total ordering on ray termination and scatter-gather operations. Condition (2)
ensures that any numerical drift between GPU and CPU paths is bounded strictly
below the chosen tolerance, preventing divergent state evolution. Since the
engine's update rules are deterministic given ordered operations and bounded
numerical deviation, the mapping from $(\bm{x}_0, n_{\text{eff}}, \sigma)$ to
$F$ is unique. Therefore, repeated executions with identical inputs yield
bitwise identical outputs.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded Numerical Stability Under CL-DIV]
Let $\Phi$ be the optical path function satisfying the eikonal equation
\[
|\nabla \Phi| = n_{\text{eff}}(\bm{x},\lambda),
\]
and let ray trajectories be integrated via RK4 with local truncation error
$\tau$. Suppose:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The Cash--Karp embedded pair enforces $\tau \le \epsilon$, where 
    $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$.
    \item The Unified Stress Scalar satisfies $S < 1.0$ throughout the run.
\end{enumerate}
Then the numerical solution $\Phi_{\text{num}}$ remains within a bounded error
neighborhood of the true solution $\Phi_{\text{true}}$:
\[
\|\Phi_{\text{num}} - \Phi_{\text{true}}\| \le C \epsilon
\]
for some constant $C$ depending on the integration interval and smoothness of 
$n_{\text{eff}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The Cash--Karp scheme provides an embedded error estimate per step, and the 
condition $\tau \le \epsilon$ ensures that local truncation error is uniformly 
bounded. Standard results from numerical analysis of RK methods imply that the 
global error is bounded by a constant multiple of the local error, yielding 
$\|\Phi_{\text{num}} - \Phi_{\text{true}}\| \le C \epsilon$. The constraint 
$S < 1.0$ guarantees that no instability regime (e.g., VRAM exhaustion, 
unbounded gradients, or TDR events) is entered, so the integration remains 
within the assumptions required for the error bound to hold. Hence, the 
numerical solution is globally stable within a controlled error neighborhood.
\end{proof}